Saito S, Koya Y, Kajiyama H, Yamashita M, Kikkawa F, Nawa A. Folate‐appended cyclodextrin carrier targets ovarian cancer cells expressing the proton‐coupled folate transporter. Cancer Sci. 2020;111:1794--1804. 10.1111/cas.14379

1. INTRODUCTION {#cas14379-sec-0001}
===============

Ovarian cancer is the leading cause of deaths from gynecologic malignancies worldwide.[1](#cas14379-bib-0001){ref-type="ref"} Most ovarian cancer patients are diagnosed at an advanced stage with widely metastatic disease within the peritoneal cavity and are treated with chemotherapy with platinum‐based drugs after cytoreductive surgery.[2](#cas14379-bib-0002){ref-type="ref"} For patients with epithelial ovarian cancer (EOC), paclitaxel (PTX) is generally used as a first‐line chemotherapeutic agent in combination with platinum‐based drugs. Due to its poor water solubility, PTX is formulated in a mixture of Cremophor‐EL and ethanol, resulting in adverse reactions such as neutropenia.[3](#cas14379-bib-0003){ref-type="ref"} Therefore, a safe formulation of PTX must be developed to improve anticancer treatment.

Cyclodextrin (CyD), a natural cyclic oligosaccharide produced from starch, is readily soluble in water and can increase the water solubility of various hydrophobic molecules by inclusion in their hydrophobic cavity.[4](#cas14379-bib-0004){ref-type="ref"}, [5](#cas14379-bib-0005){ref-type="ref"} Alcaro *et al* reported that PTX in CyD not only exhibited higher water solubility but was also more thermodynamically stable,[6](#cas14379-bib-0006){ref-type="ref"} suggesting that CyD would be a suitable replacement for Cremophor‐EL. In addition, when modified with tumor‐targeting ligands such as folate, CyD can be used as agents for molecularly target therapies.[7](#cas14379-bib-0007){ref-type="ref"}

Folate, an essential water‐soluble vitamin that is abundant in dark leafy vegetables, is required by proliferating cells as a one‐carbon source for DNA biosynthesis, repair and methylation.[8](#cas14379-bib-0008){ref-type="ref"} Rapidly dividing cancer cells require an abundant supply of folate to support their metabolic needs, including accelerated DNA biosynthesis.[9](#cas14379-bib-0009){ref-type="ref"} Folate receptor alpha (FRα), a glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)‐anchored cell surface protein that binds folate with high affinity and coordinates its transport, is overexpressed in cancers of the ovary, breast, pleura, lung, cervix, endometrium, kidney, bladder and brain.[10](#cas14379-bib-0010){ref-type="ref"} Importantly, FRα expression in normal tissues is restricted to the luminal side of tissues such as kidney and lung, and, except in kidneys, FRα is not exposed to folate in the circulatory system.[11](#cas14379-bib-0011){ref-type="ref"}, [12](#cas14379-bib-0012){ref-type="ref"} Therefore, to target FRα‐positive cancers, many types of folate‐conjugated drugs and antifolates have been developed in addition to CyD.[7](#cas14379-bib-0007){ref-type="ref"}, [13](#cas14379

